#: ../../../guide/performance.md:1
msgid "Performance"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/performance.md:3
msgid ""
"GPU performance is usually limited by memory bandwidth, compute throughput, "
"or occupancy. Occupancy is how much work can remain resident on the GPU at "
"once; too little can leave hardware idle. Tile shape and memory access "
"patterns affect all three limits."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/performance.md:8
msgid ""
"The GPU performance triangle showing memory bandwidth, compute utilization, "
"and occupancy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/performance.md:13
msgid "Start from a comparable kernel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/performance.md:15
msgid ""
"Start with the same algorithm, tensor layouts, dtypes, accumulation "
"precision, and output contract as the workload you need. The [reusable "
"kernel catalog](https://github.com/NVIDIA/cuda-rust/tree/main/cutile-rs/"
"cutile-kernels) includes GEMM memory-access variants, causal FP16 attention "
"and routed FP16 MoE, as well as fused QKV and block-scaled NVFP4 MoE. Its "
"examples demonstrate launch contracts and correctness checks. Published "
"implementations such as [NVIDIA TileGym](https://github.com/NVIDIA/TileGym) "
"are also useful algorithmic starting points. Check licenses before adapting "
"source; use APIs from the docs matching your installed cuTile Rust version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/performance.md:25
msgid ""
"Choose a relevant implementation rather than copying a configuration table "
"for another device. A short-sequence attention kernel can avoid a streaming "
"loop; a small MoE batch can gather routed rows directly, while larger "
"batches may benefit from materializing a packed input. Include routing, "
"packing and combine costs when comparing complete operations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/performance.md:31
msgid "Memory access and IR structure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/performance.md:33
msgid ""
"Tensor partitions provide bounds handling and layout metadata. A tensor-"
"derived loop bound such as `num_tiles(&part_a, 1i32)` also expresses how the "
"traversal relates to its input. Prefer this over separately calculating the "
"tile count when both describe the same loop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/performance.md:38
msgid "Existing APIs expose several independent choices:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/performance.md:40
msgid ""
"`load_view_tko` can select `tma::Disabled` for non-TMA tensor loads while "
"retaining tensor padding. Establish partition access with "
"`check_partition_access` when calling this low-level operation directly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/performance.md:43
msgid ""
"`load_ptr_tko` supports masked gathers with scalar padding, including "
"multidimensional pointer tiles. See [pointer loads](masked-pointer-loads)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/performance.md:45
msgid ""
"`permute` can express transposed MMA accumulation, followed by an output "
"transpose. This changes the tile layout and can change the generated "
"schedule."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/performance.md:47
msgid ""
"Load-latency hints and `CompileOptions::num_cta_in_cga` expose scheduling "
"choices on supported targets. They are candidates to measure, not universal "
"defaults."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/performance.md:50
msgid ""
"Hoist invariant pointer bases, row offsets and masks out of inner loops "
"where possible. Widen integer operands before multiplying offsets. Preserve "
"masks for partial tiles and use actual strides; a contiguous row-major "
"formula is valid only under that layout contract. Raw pointers transfer "
"these obligations to the caller, including synchronization and lifetime "
"requirements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/performance.md:56
msgid ""
"The shape of a gather, intermediate reshapes, padding, and placement of "
"address arithmetic can affect backend scheduling. Inspect the emitted Tile "
"IR rather than assuming two equivalent formulas produce the same "
"instructions. Measure both forms before preferring a flat or "
"multidimensional gather. Removing checks or adding `in_bounds` assertions is "
"not a general optimization: every such assertion needs a proof for all "
"supported shapes, including tails."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/performance.md:63
msgid "Tile Shape"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/performance.md:65
msgid ""
"Tile size controls how much work each tile block performs. Larger tiles "
"improve data reuse and reduce launch overhead per element, but they also "
"consume more registers and can reduce occupancy."
msgstr ""

